WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.003
The dataset.

2
00:00:01.283 --> 00:00:02.605
Start with what you have.

3
00:00:02.885 --> 00:00:09.883
Your Studio 2 project produced a corpus, a question-answering system, and probably a handful of test questions.

4
00:00:10.163 --> 00:00:17.267
Grow that into a set with four kinds of cases, and tag each case with its kind and source so you can report by slice.

5
00:00:17.547 --> 00:00:22.603
Real questions, from users, logs, or colleagues who would actually use the system.

6
00:00:22.883 --> 00:00:30.925
Synthetic questions generated from documents (lesson 11), checked by hand, especially for questions that need two chunks.

7
00:00:31.205 --> 00:00:37.093
Regression cases: every failure you found in error analysis and review, with the rule it broke.

8
00:00:37.373 --> 00:00:43.325
Should-abstain cases: questions the corpus can’t answer, where the right behavior is to say so.

9
00:00:43.605 --> 00:00:48.661
Without these, a system that always answers looks better than one that knows its limits.

10
00:00:48.941 --> 00:00:50.499
Each case needs labels.

11
00:00:50.779 --> 00:01:00.037
At minimum: the relevant chunks (for retrieval metrics), and either a reference answer or a list of facts a correct answer must contain (for correctness).

12
00:01:00.317 --> 00:01:03.603
Add a should-abstain flag, and notes from whoever labeled it.

13
00:01:03.883 --> 00:01:14.187
Labels are the expensive part, so spend them where they matter: a domain expert on reference answers, synthetic generation plus spot checks for retrieval labels.

14
00:01:14.467 --> 00:01:15.491
Split the set.

15
00:01:15.771 --> 00:01:24.837
A dev set you iterate against freely, and a held-out set you run rarely (say, once per candidate you are seriously considering) and never tune against.

16
00:01:25.117 --> 00:01:34.205
Version both, as files in the repository with a version number in the log, because a number without its dataset version can’t be compared to anything.

17
00:01:34.485 --> 00:01:42.251
When chunking changes, regenerate retrieval labels with a script, not by hand, and record that as a new dataset version.

18
00:01:42.531 --> 00:01:43.384
How big?

19
00:01:43.664 --> 00:01:47.419
Large enough that the differences you care about show up outside the noise.

20
00:01:47.699 --> 00:01:50.344
That is a statistics question, covered below.
